direction: down

title: |md
  ## CO-RE relocation: kernel A vs kernel B
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

# Kernel A side
ka: "Kernel A (build host)" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  layout_a: |md
    `struct task_struct` layout (kernel A):
    ```
    offset  field
    0       state
    24      stack
    32      flags
    ...
    1872    real_parent  ← here
    1880    parent
    ...
    2384    tgid
    2388    pid
    ```
  |
}

# Kernel B side (drifted layout)
kb: "Kernel B (target host, different version)" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  layout_b: |md
    `struct task_struct` layout (kernel B):
    ```
    offset  field
    0       state
    24      stack
    32      flags
    ...
    1920    real_parent  ← moved (+48 bytes)
    1928    parent
    ...
    2440    tgid          ← moved (+56 bytes)
    2444    pid
    ```
  |
}

# the .bpf.o (single artifact)
obj: "hello.bpf.o (built once on kernel A)" {
  shape: package
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  src: |md
    Source:
    ```c
    BPF_CORE_READ(task, real_parent, tgid);
    ```
  |
  emitted: |md
    Emitted instructions:
    ```
    r1 = task
    r1 = *(u64 *)(r1 + 0xCO_RE)   ← real_parent
    r1 = *(u32 *)(r1 + 0xCO_RE)   ← tgid
    ```
    + CO-RE relocation records:
    ```
    relo: kind=field_byte_offset
          type="task_struct"
          field="real_parent"
    relo: kind=field_byte_offset
          type="task_struct"
          field="tgid"
    ```
  |
}

ka -> obj: "compile against\nkernel A's vmlinux.h"

# At load time
load_a: "Load on kernel A:\nlibbpf patches\n+1872 and +2384" {style.fill: "#dcfce7"}
load_b: "Load on kernel B:\nlibbpf patches\n+1920 and +2440" {style.fill: "#dcfce7"}

obj -> load_a
obj -> load_b

ka.layout_a -> load_a: "field offsets\nfrom A's BTF"
kb.layout_b -> load_b: "field offsets\nfrom B's BTF"

note: |md
  **Same `.bpf.o`, two different patched offsets at load time.**

  Without CO-RE, you'd have to recompile for every kernel. With CO-RE, you ship one `.o` and it adapts.
| {near: bottom-center; shape: text}
